\title[Convexity and monotonicity of EML activations]{Global Convexity and Sharp Monotonicity Domains for Generalized Exponential--Logarithmic Activations, and their Dequantization to Tropical Addition}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{5 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the two-parameter family of \emph{generalized exponential--logarithmic} (EML) activation functions
$E_{a,b}(x) = a x + \log(1 + e^{bx})$, $(a,b)\in\R^2$,
which contains the softplus ($a=0$, $b=1$), the leaky/residual softplus ($a>0$), and---after rescaling---all smoothed rectifiers. We determine the exact parameter domain on which these functions are strictly monotone and strictly convex. Writing $\sigma(t)=e^t/(1+e^t)$ for the logistic function, we prove the closed forms $E_{a,b}'(x) = a + b\sigma(bx)$ and $E_{a,b}''(x)=b^2\sigma(bx)(1-\sigma(bx))$, from which we obtain: (i) \emph{global strict convexity} of $E_{a,b}$ on all of $\R$ for \emph{every} $a\in\R$ and \emph{every} $b\neq0$; and (ii) the \emph{sharp monotonicity criterion}: for $b>0$, $E_{a,b}$ is strictly increasing on $\R$ if and only if $a\geq 0$, necessity being established by evaluating the derivative limit at $-\infty$.

We then exhibit a bridge to idempotent algebra. The same exponential--logarithmic pairing defines the log-sum-exp operation $x\oplus_b y = b^{-1}\log(e^{bx}+e^{by})$, which we show is \emph{exactly}---not asymptotically---commutative, associative and right-distributive over ordinary addition, being the transport of $(\R_{>0},+,\times)$ along the isomorphism $x\mapsto e^{bx}$. The unique failing tropical axiom, idempotency, fails by an exactly computable constant: $x\oplus_b x = x + (\log 2)/b$. We prove the sharp sandwich $\max(x,y) < x\oplus_b y \leq \max(x,y)+(\log2)/b$ and deduce Maslov dequantization, $x\oplus_b y\to\max(x,y)$ as $b\to\infty$, together with its min-plus mirror image. Combining both halves yields a \emph{bridge theorem}: the rescaled activation $S_b(x)=E_{0,b}(x)/b$ is strictly convex and strictly increasing for every $b>0$, strictly dominates $\max(x,0)$, approximates it uniformly in $x$ with error at most $(\log2)/b$, and converges to it as $b\to\infty$---at which point both strictness properties are lost.
\end{abstract}

